---
import "../../styles/getting-started.css";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import GettingStartedPager from "../../components/GettingStartedPager.astro";
import { GHARCHIVE_REPO } from "../../data/gharchive";

const description =
  "Let a coding agent build a lake of GitHub activity on magmalake: install the skills for Mojo, magmalake, mojoshelf and AWS, and give it the prompt that built mojo-census.";

const SKILLS = "https://github.com/magmalake/skills";

const prompt = `Build me a lake of GitHub activity with magmalake, and an app that shows how
Mojo libraries have grown.

- Make a new pixi project with orca (\`pixi shelf add orca-mojo\`). Use the
  magmalake skills (orca, dista, magmalake-apps), mojoshelf's skills and
  Modular's mojo-syntax.
- gharchive: a daily pipeline that imports each day of the GitHub Archive
  (data.gharchive.org, 24 hourly .json.gz files) into an Iceberg table
  gharchive.events (id, type, created_at, repo, actor, the event's payload
  as JSON), partitioned by day, from 2023-01-01, every day at 03:00 UTC for
  the day before.
- mojo_repos: a sensor on gharchive.events that finds Mojo repositories
  (pushes touching .mojo or .🔥 files, and projects whose pixi.toml or
  mojoproject.toml names a Mojo package) and keeps them in mojo.repos (when
  GitHub says each was created, when it was first seen, how it was found,
  stars, forks, license, default branch, description).
- mojo_history: keyed by repository, 50 a run, started by new repositories:
  clone each one in a dista task and read its history into mojo.commits
  (lines of Mojo at each commit, which tooling the project used: none, then
  magic, then pixi, and the Mojo version its lock file or manifest named)
  and mojo.migrations (each change of tooling or Mojo version). Versions
  sort in one order across Modular's renumbering (24.x, 25.x, 0.25.x,
  0.26.x, 1.x); test that.
- The console: a flare app over those tables: the libraries by size and
  age, the Mojo versions they moved to over time, a page per repository.
- Run it locally first on a few hours of the archive. Then deploy it to AWS
  with \`orca aws\` (on-demand workers, autoscaling from 0), backfill one
  month to see what it costs, then the rest, and serve the console on its
  own hostname. The tables go in the stack's public bucket.`;
---

<Base title="Let an agent build it — Getting started" description={description}>
  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide docs gs">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a></p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Let an agent build it</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A coding agent can write all of <a href={GHARCHIVE_REPO} rel="noopener">mojo-census</a>
        if it knows current Mojo, the magmalake libraries and AWS. Each of those comes as a
        skill, and AWS also as an MCP server. The commands below are for Claude Code; any
        agent that reads
        <a href="https://agentskills.io/specification" rel="noopener">Agent Skills</a> can
        use the <code>npx skills</code> lines instead, which need
        <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/download" rel="noopener">Node.js</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#skills">Install the skills</a>
        <a href="#prompt">The prompt</a>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="skills">Install the skills</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The Mojo skills</strong> come in one install. magmalake's
        <code>magmalake</code> plugin holds
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/orca`} rel="noopener">orca</a>,
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/dista`} rel="noopener">dista</a>,
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/magmalake-apps`} rel="noopener">magmalake-apps</a>
        (a whole project, its lake, a flare app and <code>orca aws</code>) and
        <a href={`${SKILLS}/tree/main/mojo-data-performance`} rel="noopener">mojo-data-performance</a>
        (a pipeline's per-row loops and the readers, writers and codecs under them, and how
        to take a benchmark number that survives being checked). It also brings two skills
        written by others:
        <a href="https://github.com/modular/skills" rel="noopener">Modular's</a>
        <code>mojo-syntax</code>, which keeps the agent writing the Mojo of today rather than
        the Mojo it was trained on, and
        <a href="https://github.com/mojoshelf/mojoshelf" rel="noopener">mojoshelf's</a>
        <code>mojoshelf-consume</code>, which tells it how to find tins and add them to a
        project. In Claude Code 2.1.275 or later:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Claude Code command" code={`/plugin install magmalake --marketplace magmalake/skills`} />
      <p>In an older Claude Code:</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Claude Code commands" code={`/plugin marketplace add magmalake/skills
/plugin install magmalake@magmalake`} />
      <p>In any other agent, such as Codex or Cursor, this installs all six:</p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`npx skills add magmalake/skills`} />
      <p>
        <strong>AWS</strong> publishes the
        <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/agent-toolkit/latest/userguide/quick-start.html" rel="noopener">Agent Toolkit for AWS</a>:
        the AWS MCP Server, through which the agent reads AWS documentation and runs AWS
        calls with your credentials, and a set of AWS skills. It needs
        <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" rel="noopener">uv</a>, and the AWS CLI signed in
        (<code>aws login</code>) for anything beyond reading documentation. In Claude Code
        one plugin brings both:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Claude Code commands" code={`/plugin install aws-core@claude-plugins-official
/reload-plugins`} />
      <p>
        Elsewhere, add the server to the agent's MCP configuration and install the skills.
        This is AWS's line for Claude Code with your own credentials; the
        <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/agent-toolkit/latest/userguide/getting-started-aws-mcp-server.html" rel="noopener">setup guide</a>
        has the other agents', and the options for sign-in in the browser and for more than one
        profile. Set <code>AWS_REGION</code> to your project's Region:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`claude mcp add-json aws-mcp '{"type":"stdio","command":"uvx","args":["mcp-proxy-for-aws-cli@latest","https://aws-mcp.us-east-1.api.aws/mcp","--metadata","AWS_REGION=us-east-2"],"env":{}}'
npx skills add aws/agent-toolkit-for-aws/skills`} />

      <h2 id="prompt">The prompt</h2>
      <p>
        Start the agent in an empty directory and give it this. It names the skills to use,
        the three pipelines and their tables, the console, and the order to work in: locally
        on a few hours of the archive first, then on AWS.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="prompt" code={prompt} />
      <p>
        The agent that wrote <a href={GHARCHIVE_REPO} rel="noopener">mojo-census</a>
        from this prompt ran it locally on two hours of 2024-06-12: 476,227 events (154 MB of
        Parquet), 23 candidate repositories, 4 of them Mojo, and 3 histories read, with 372
        commits and 27 changes of tooling or version. It also kept a list of where the skills
        and libraries were wrong or silent,
        <a href={`${GHARCHIVE_REPO}/blob/main/GAPS.md`} rel="noopener">GAPS.md</a>, and the
        skills and libraries have since been corrected from it. Expect your agent to take its
        own route to the same tables; <a href="/getting-started/step-by-step">Step by step</a>
        walks through the route this one took.
      </p>

      <GettingStartedPager
        prev={{ href: "/getting-started", label: "Getting started" }}
        next={{ href: "/getting-started/step-by-step", label: "Step by step" }}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
